WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.597
Here's a recipe for tomato and egg stir-fry.

00:00:03.597 --> 00:00:06.443
First the ingredients, then the steps.

00:00:06.443 --> 00:00:07.717
Cut the tomatoes.

00:00:07.717 --> 00:00:08.766
Beat the eggs.

00:00:08.766 --> 00:00:09.740
Heat the oil.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.389
Now imagine you cook this every day.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:15.837
You wouldn't write out how to beat the eggs each time.

00:00:15.837 --> 00:00:20.370
You'd write it once, give it a name, and after that, just say the name.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.735
Programs work the same way.

00:00:22.735 --> 00:00:26.540
Some paragraphs of code are needed again and again.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:32.360
Python lets you give such a paragraph a name, and run it by writing that name.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.280
A named, reusable paragraph of code is called a function.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:45.787
Welcome to Lecture 4 of Introduction to AI Programming: Functions, Strings and Files.

00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:51.764
In this first part, we'll define our own functions, see what Python does when it calls one,

00:00:51.764 --> 00:00:59.617
and learn the difference that trips up more beginners than anything else in this lecture: printing a value versus returning it.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.419
Here's the smallest example.

00:01:02.419 --> 00:01:07.320
The function is called hello, and its body has two print statements.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:13.230
Below the definition, we call hello, print a line of our own, and call hello again.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:19.431
The output shows Hello and Funny, then the middle line, then Hello and Funny again.

00:01:19.431 --> 00:01:23.660
The two lines inside hello were written once, and ran twice.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.451
A quick note on names.

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:28.988
The slides call this function Hello, with a capital H.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:35.930
Python's style guide uses lowercase names, with words joined by underscores, so in this course it's hello.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.350
There are two kinds of function.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:46.966
Built-in functions come with Python: print, input, int, float, len, max, min, and many more.

00:01:46.966 --> 00:01:50.940
And there are the functions we define ourselves.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:56.210
We call a function by writing its name, then parentheses with the input inside.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.794
Max of the string 'Hello world' gives back the largest character, which is the letter 'w'.

00:02:02.794 --> 00:02:07.920
Min gives back the smallest one, which is the space between the two words.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.750
The value we pass in is called an argument.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.310
The value the call gives back is its result.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:16.892
One warning.

00:02:16.892 --> 00:02:21.476
Don't use the name of a built-in function as a variable.

00:02:21.476 --> 00:02:26.550
After max equals 5, the name max no longer means the function.

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:30.173
Now let's look at a definition closely.

00:02:30.173 --> 00:02:36.653
It starts with the keyword def, then a name, then parameters in parentheses, and a colon.

00:02:37.003 --> 00:02:39.180
That first line is the signature.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.883
The indented lines below it are the body.

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:46.443
When Python runs a def statement, it does three things.

00:02:46.443 --> 00:02:49.658
It creates a function with that signature.

00:02:49.658 --> 00:02:53.484
It sets the function's body to the indented lines.

00:02:53.484 --> 00:02:58.153
And it binds the name to that function, in the current frame.

00:02:58.753 --> 00:03:01.616
So def is a kind of assignment.

00:03:01.616 --> 00:03:08.725
It binds a name to a function, just like x equals 5 binds a name to a number.

00:03:08.725 --> 00:03:11.033
It does not run the body.

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:14.206
Here's a program that shows it.

00:03:14.206 --> 00:03:22.393
It sets x to 5, prints Hello, and defines a function called print_lyrics, which prints two lines of a song.

00:03:22.743 --> 00:03:25.328
Then it prints the word Yo.

00:03:25.328 --> 00:03:28.103
It adds 2 to x, and prints x.

00:03:28.453 --> 00:03:30.157
Follow Python through it.

00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:34.111
The def line only creates the function and binds the name.

00:03:34.111 --> 00:03:36.973
Python skips over the body and carries on.

00:03:37.323 --> 00:03:39.942
So the output is Hello, Yo, and 7.

00:03:39.942 --> 00:03:44.563
The lyrics never appear, because nobody called the function.

00:03:44.913 --> 00:03:50.713
Now add one line: a call to print_lyrics, right after Yo.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:55.463
This time, the two lines of the song come out between Yo and 7.

00:03:56.233 --> 00:03:58.996
So what exactly happens at that call?

00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:00.713
Let's watch the frames.

00:04:01.063 --> 00:04:04.961
The program's own variables live in the global frame.

00:04:04.961 --> 00:04:12.903
After the first lines, the global frame holds x, equal to 5, and the name print_lyrics, bound to a function.

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:17.925
When Python reaches the call, it creates a new frame for it.

00:04:17.925 --> 00:04:22.051
A frame is a fresh area for the call's own variables.

00:04:22.051 --> 00:04:26.333
Then it jumps into the body and runs it, top to bottom.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:34.373
When the body ends, Python throws that frame away, and continues with the line after the call.

00:04:34.973 --> 00:04:37.810
You'll see these frames again and again.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.853
In Lecture 6, recursion is built on exactly this picture.

00:04:42.203 --> 00:04:43.467
And one pitfall.

00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:48.366
A function must be defined before the line that calls it runs.

00:04:48.366 --> 00:04:51.763
Call it too early, and you get a NameError.

00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:54.731
Most functions need some input.

00:04:54.731 --> 00:04:59.197
An argument is a value we pass into a function when we call it.

00:04:59.197 --> 00:05:03.733
Arguments let one function do different work on different calls.

00:05:04.083 --> 00:05:08.323
A parameter is a variable in the function definition.

00:05:08.323 --> 00:05:11.763
Here, greet has one parameter, called lang.

00:05:12.113 --> 00:05:18.237
When we call greet with the string 'es', Python makes a new frame,

00:05:18.237 --> 00:05:22.877
and binds the parameter lang to the argument 'es'.

00:05:22.877 --> 00:05:25.753
Inside that call, lang is 'es'.

00:05:26.353 --> 00:05:29.116
So greet of 'es' prints Hola.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:31.880
Greet of 'fr' prints Bonjour.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.833
And anything else prints Hello.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.006
Often a function computes something and hands it back.

00:05:39.006 --> 00:05:40.520
That's what return does.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:46.730
The return statement ends the function, and sends a value back to the caller.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:52.590
The call expression then has that value, just as len of 'hi' has the value 2.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.366
This greet returns the string 'Hello'.

00:05:56.366 --> 00:06:00.044
So print(greet, 'Glenn') prints Hello Glenn.

00:06:00.044 --> 00:06:03.470
The call greet was replaced by its value.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.734
A function that returns a value is called a fruitful function.

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:14.740
Here's greet with a language parameter again, but now each branch returns its greeting, instead of printing it.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.339
And return ends the call at once.

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:22.450
As soon as the line 'return Hola' has run, the rest of the body is skipped.

00:06:23.234 --> 00:06:26.435
Print and return both seem to give back a result.

00:06:26.435 --> 00:06:29.114
But they're different, and here's a test.

00:06:29.464 --> 00:06:31.826
Greet print prints its greeting.

00:06:31.826 --> 00:06:33.597
Greet return returns it.

00:06:33.597 --> 00:06:35.664
Everything else is the same.

00:06:36.014 --> 00:06:38.122
Pause the video and predict.

00:06:38.122 --> 00:06:41.134
What does each of these two lines print?

00:06:41.684 --> 00:06:45.724
The first line prints 'Hola Sally', as you'd expect.

00:06:46.074 --> 00:06:48.701
The second line prints two lines.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.532
First Hola, on its own.

00:06:50.532 --> 00:06:54.354
Then, on the next line, None, followed by Sally.

00:06:55.134 --> 00:06:56.254
Let's see why.

00:06:56.254 --> 00:07:03.934
Before the outer print can run, Python must evaluate its argument, the call greet_print of 'es'.

00:07:04.284 --> 00:07:07.686
That call prints Hola, on a line of its own.

00:07:07.686 --> 00:07:11.244
Then its body ends without a return statement.

00:07:11.594 --> 00:07:20.354
A function that ends without return still returns something: the special value None, which means no value.

00:07:20.954 --> 00:07:27.554
Only then does the outer print run, with the two values None and Sally.

00:07:27.904 --> 00:07:30.474
Here's the difference side by side.

00:07:30.474 --> 00:07:35.174
Print shows a value on the screen, and only a human can read it.

00:07:35.174 --> 00:07:41.784
Return hands the value back to the program, which can store it, compare it, or pass it on.

00:07:42.134 --> 00:07:44.652
Print doesn't end the function.

00:07:44.652 --> 00:07:46.520
Return ends it at once.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.094
And a call with no return evaluates to None.

00:07:50.444 --> 00:07:54.062
You can see it as soon as you use the result.

00:07:54.062 --> 00:07:57.520
Greet_return of 'es' equals 'Hola' is True.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:06.044
But greet_print of 'es' equals 'Hola' prints Hola, and then gives False, because the call's value is None.

00:08:06.394 --> 00:08:11.102
So if a function's job is to compute a value, return it.

00:08:11.102 --> 00:08:14.634
Let the caller decide whether to print it.

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:18.954
A function that returns a value is fruitful.

00:08:18.954 --> 00:08:22.074
A function that doesn't is called void.

00:08:22.074 --> 00:08:23.834
Its calls return None.

00:08:24.184 --> 00:08:27.652
Print_lyrics and greet_print are void.

00:08:27.652 --> 00:08:31.304
Greet_return, max, and len are fruitful.

00:08:31.654 --> 00:08:36.569
Void functions are fine when the effect is the point, like printing a report.

00:08:36.569 --> 00:08:44.294
The slides' grade example is one: print_grade prints the letter grade for a score, and main reads the score and calls it.

00:08:44.644 --> 00:08:49.864
One change from the slide: it reads the score with eval(input).

00:08:49.864 --> 00:08:51.935
Use float(input) instead.

00:08:51.935 --> 00:08:57.404
Eval runs whatever the user types as Python code, which is unsafe.

00:08:58.201 --> 00:08:59.561
Let's sum up.

00:08:59.911 --> 00:09:03.095
Def creates a function and binds its name.

00:09:03.095 --> 00:09:04.991
It does not run the body.

00:09:05.341 --> 00:09:13.941
A call creates a new frame, binds the parameters to the arguments, runs the body, and evaluates to the return value.

00:09:14.291 --> 00:09:16.823
Return hands a value back.

00:09:16.823 --> 00:09:18.772
Print only shows it.

00:09:18.772 --> 00:09:22.571
A function without return returns None.

00:09:22.921 --> 00:09:24.208
Here's one to try.

00:09:24.208 --> 00:09:28.286
Change print_grade into a fruitful function called grade,

00:09:28.286 --> 00:09:31.720
which returns the letter instead of printing it.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.081
Then check that grade of 85 is B.

00:09:34.631 --> 00:09:41.673
In part two, we'll give functions several parameters, default values and keyword arguments,

00:09:41.673 --> 00:09:45.311
and see which frame a variable really lives in.

